NAME
    su - change this session's user identity

SYNOPSIS
    su <uid>[:<gid>]

DESCRIPTION
    Sets the shell's own uid and/or gid (numeric - there is no /etc/passwd yet,
    so user names are not resolved). A builtin, because it changes the shell's
    OWN task identity: a /bin program would only change its short-lived child.
    Commands spawned afterward inherit the new identity, and the prompt switches
    between # (root) and $ (a normal user).

    Forms, like chown:
        uid        set the user, leave the group
        uid:gid    set both
        :gid       set the group, leave the user
        uid:       set the user, leave the group (same as uid)

    Only ROOT (uid 0) may change identity. Root can drop to any user; a non-root
    session cannot change its uid at all - there is no way back up without
    authentication (which is the login step, not yet built), so this is a
    one-way drop. That is the whole privilege rule for now: no escalation.

EXAMPLES
    su 1000            become uid 1000, keep the current group
    su 1000:1000       become uid 1000, gid 1000
    su :20             change only the group to 20

SEE ALSO
    id, sh
